{"name":"Abundance","hex":"#629763","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"abundance","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large quantity; many. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","An overflowing fullness or ample sufficiency; profusion; copious supply; superfluity; plentifulness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/abundance"}],"entries":[{"title":"Abundance (ecology)","blocks":[{"text":"In ecology, local abundance is the relative representation of a species in a particular ecosystem. It is usually measured as the number of individuals found per sample. The ratio of abundance of one species to one or multiple other species living in an ecosystem is referred to as relative species abundances. Both indicators are relevant for computing biodiversity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Effect_of_herbivore_abundance_in_seagrass_meadows.jpg/330px-Effect_of_herbivore_abundance_in_seagrass_meadows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_(ecology)"},{"title":"Abundance (Klein and Thompson book)","blocks":[{"text":"Abundance is a nonfiction book by Ezra Klein and Derek Thompson published by Avid Reader Press in March 2025. The book examines the reasons behind the lack of progress on ambitious industrial strategy projects in the United States, including those related to affordable housing, infrastructure, and climate change. It became a New York Times Best Seller and popularized the YIMBY movement."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/32/Cover_of_Abundance_Klein_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_(Klein_and_Thompson_book)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Effect_of_herbivore_abundance_in_seagrass_meadows.jpg/500px-Effect_of_herbivore_abundance_in_s